WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.367
-Our next guest
is a fantastic comedian

00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:04.300
who used to write here
at "Late Night."

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.701
Her book, "Unreliable Narrator:

00:00:05.701 --> 00:00:07.567
Me, Myself, and Impostor
Syndrome"

00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:09.601
is on sale now.
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:09.601 --> 00:00:11.734
Aparna Nancherla, everybody.

00:00:11.734 --> 00:00:12.934
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:12.934 --> 00:00:19.434
♪♪

00:00:19.434 --> 00:00:25.934
♪♪

00:00:25.934 --> 00:00:27.501
Hi, Aparna.
-Hey!

00:00:27.501 --> 00:00:29.000
-I'm so happy
to have you back here.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.267
You wrote on the show, 2015.
-Allegedly.

00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:35.234
-Allegedly.
-Allegedly. Yes.

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:38.267
-You did have an A-plus joke
that you remember fondly.

00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:40.434
-I do.
It was a visual joke,

00:00:40.434 --> 00:00:42.200
which means, you know,
the punch line

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.000
is a reveal of a picture.
And you know me.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.801
I love pictures.
-You do love pictures.

00:00:46.801 --> 00:00:49.000
That's what we always
said about you.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.234
-But yes.
-We dug it up

00:00:50.234 --> 00:00:51.767
and we would love to show it.

00:00:51.767 --> 00:00:53.534
-Okay, great.
-Because I will say this.

00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:55.767
It's one of those great jokes,
it ages very well.

00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:57.934
-Okay, great.
-I should let our audience

00:00:57.934 --> 00:00:59.601
know this is pre-pandemic
if you're wondering

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:01.534
why I'm dressed like
an actual talk show host.

00:01:01.534 --> 00:01:02.534
[ Laughter ]

00:01:02.534 --> 00:01:04.634
Here's Aparna's wonderful joke.

00:01:04.634 --> 00:01:07.667
Pope Francis began his tour
of the United States today,

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:09.868
and he kicked it off
in style with a stop

00:01:09.868 --> 00:01:12.467
at his favorite restaurant,
Pope Yes.

00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:18.300
[ Laughter, cheers, applause ]

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.534
-I -- Okay.

00:01:21.534 --> 00:01:24.167
I remember because, you know,
we run the jokes ahead

00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:27.834
in the meeting during the day
and the look in your eyes

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:31.167
when you saw the sign
Popeyes and you were like,

00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:33.534
"Oh, Pope Yes,"
and I was like, "Yeah!"

00:01:33.534 --> 00:01:34.834
[ Laughter ]

00:01:34.834 --> 00:01:37.100
I was so excited because,
you know, monologue writer,

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.501
you write jokes every day.

00:01:38.501 --> 00:01:41.667
And, like, the thing about me
that I quickly learned,

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:45.567
I only have about
one or two good ideas a week.

00:01:45.567 --> 00:01:48.501
and then the rest of the week
I celebrate those ideas.

00:01:48.501 --> 00:01:51.767
So, you know, I was really
struggling to come up.

00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:53.067
And that Pope Yes, I was like,

00:01:53.067 --> 00:01:55.267
"I didn't believe
in God before, but..."

00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:56.567
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:59.067
-Pope Yes.
-Pope Yes. Pope Yes.

00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:01.400
-Now, when you were on the show,

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.100
did you ever think
you would come back

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.501
and be a guest on the show?

00:02:04.501 --> 00:02:07.234
-No, not under
positive circumstances.

00:02:07.234 --> 00:02:10.000
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.601
-You thought we would drag you
back in here

00:02:11.601 --> 00:02:13.501
for an investigation?

00:02:13.501 --> 00:02:16.834
-Well, it was like, you know,
"she got canceled inevitably.

00:02:16.834 --> 00:02:18.667
Let's find out more."

00:02:18.667 --> 00:02:20.467
-Oh, yeah.
You would be canceled publicly

00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:22.367
and we'd bring you back sort of
for a public hearing.

00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:26.133
-Yes. Yes. It would be like my
first apology to the public.

00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:27.801
-I got you.
Well, I'm certainly glad

00:02:27.801 --> 00:02:29.467
we're not using
your time here for that

00:02:29.467 --> 00:02:32.234
because you have written
a fantastic book.

00:02:32.234 --> 00:02:34.767
-Thank you.
-And it is about --

00:02:34.767 --> 00:02:36.834
it is about anxiety
and depression

00:02:36.834 --> 00:02:39.167
and impostor syndrome
and self-doubt.

00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:40.968
-Yes.
-Now, even people,

00:02:40.968 --> 00:02:43.734
competent people I know
when they write books,

00:02:43.734 --> 00:02:46.400
that is when, if they've
never had self-doubt,

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.200
that's when it creeps up.
So how hard was it for you

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.100
to have that very real
feeling of self-doubt

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:55.701
to then write a book which
brings on self-doubt so often?

00:02:55.701 --> 00:02:58.000
-Yeah. I mean, I decided
to write the book

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.167
because I was struggling
with self-doubt

00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:01.968
so acutely
in my actual life.

00:03:01.968 --> 00:03:05.601
Like, I had sort of had some
moderate success as a comedian.

00:03:05.601 --> 00:03:08.067
I realized the more
opportunities I got,

00:03:08.067 --> 00:03:12.167
the worse my anxiety and, like,
impostor syndrome got.

00:03:12.167 --> 00:03:13.968
So I was like,
you know, self-doubt.

00:03:13.968 --> 00:03:16.334
If you have so much to say,
you do some work,

00:03:16.334 --> 00:03:18.400
write me -- write me a book.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.801
But the thing
with self-doubt,

00:03:21.801 --> 00:03:24.534
it's not a good collaborator.
-Right.

00:03:24.534 --> 00:03:25.801
[ Laughter ]

00:03:25.801 --> 00:03:27.501
-Like, as soon
as I started writing,

00:03:27.501 --> 00:03:29.167
it was just like,
"Who are you to write a book?"

00:03:29.167 --> 00:03:30.968
You know, it's just like...
[ Laughs ]

00:03:30.968 --> 00:03:33.234
Like, it didn't like --

00:03:33.234 --> 00:03:36.300
I tried -- In writing the book,
I tried to be like, okay,

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.901
I'm going to learn
to work with it.

00:03:37.901 --> 00:03:39.234
But, like,
even before I came out

00:03:39.234 --> 00:03:40.868
for this interview,
I was just like, you know,

00:03:40.868 --> 00:03:42.200
trying to think
of positive things.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:43.767
I was like, "Be yourself."

00:03:43.767 --> 00:03:45.968
And then my self-doubt
was just like,

00:03:45.968 --> 00:03:46.968
"Who are you?"

00:03:46.968 --> 00:03:48.567
[ Laughter ]

00:03:48.567 --> 00:03:50.634
-It's not helpful.
-Not helpful.

00:03:50.634 --> 00:03:52.767
-A thing you did, which I
imagine --

00:03:52.767 --> 00:03:54.634
I don't know if it was
because you wanted to do it

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:57.334
for the readers or you wanted to
do it for yourself,

00:03:57.334 --> 00:03:59.334
but you give
your depression a name.

00:03:59.334 --> 00:04:00.868
-Yes.
-And that was I guess

00:04:00.868 --> 00:04:03.167
you didn't want to have
to keep writing "depression."

00:04:03.167 --> 00:04:05.000
-Yeah, because, you know,

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.367
depression doesn't have
a great reputation.

00:04:07.367 --> 00:04:08.767
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:10.501
I feel like it's earned
its bad reputation.

00:04:10.501 --> 00:04:11.701
-Yes, yes, yes.

00:04:11.701 --> 00:04:13.601
But I wanted to put
some more levity in it,

00:04:13.601 --> 00:04:15.501
so I just started
calling it Brenda.

00:04:15.501 --> 00:04:18.834
-Yeah.
-Because I felt like that was

00:04:18.834 --> 00:04:20.968
the perfect amount
of, like, casual,

00:04:20.968 --> 00:04:24.167
but also you sort of are like,
"Oh, Brenda's here again."

00:04:24.167 --> 00:04:27.701
You know, like,
you're not so mad,

00:04:27.701 --> 00:04:30.467
but you're not psyched
she's there either.

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:33.067
She's kind of one of those
friends where you're like,

00:04:33.067 --> 00:04:35.234
"Do we really like each other?

00:04:35.234 --> 00:04:37.100
I don't know.
She's always here."

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.400
[ Laughter ]

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.434
-The book has
very funny moments.

00:04:42.434 --> 00:04:44.667
There's this sort of levity,
I expect,

00:04:44.667 --> 00:04:46.734
knowing you as a writer
and a stand-up,

00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:49.234
but how is writing a book like
this different from stand-up?

00:04:49.234 --> 00:04:51.000
Did you find it
completely different?

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.968
-Yeah, I mean,
I felt like -- I feel like

00:04:52.968 --> 00:04:54.133
with writing stand-up,

00:04:54.133 --> 00:04:56.033
you know, you get your setup,
your punch line,

00:04:56.033 --> 00:04:58.234
you have kind of a clean out
at the end.

00:04:58.234 --> 00:05:01.534
And I feel with a lot of these
topics like mental health

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:04.000
or body image
or like productivity,

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.601
like, there's such messy
ongoing internal topics for me

00:05:07.601 --> 00:05:09.934
that I kind of didn't
always want to give myself

00:05:09.934 --> 00:05:11.501
that out of a punch line

00:05:11.501 --> 00:05:15.434
and just kind of live
in the mess a little bit more.

00:05:15.434 --> 00:05:17.834
But now I'm noticing
in promoting the book,

00:05:17.834 --> 00:05:19.234
it's a little weird
because it's like

00:05:19.234 --> 00:05:21.167
instead of solving
my self-doubt,

00:05:21.167 --> 00:05:22.534
I just felt like it
ended the book

00:05:22.534 --> 00:05:25.067
just like covered
in my own crap.

00:05:25.067 --> 00:05:27.767
And then I'm just like, "Look at
me, I'm covered in crap.

00:05:27.767 --> 00:05:29.901
Don't you want to know why?
Read my book."

00:05:29.901 --> 00:05:33.000
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.434
-You've talked about
being on stage,

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:37.400
and obviously a lot
of stand-up comedians

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:39.868
when they're trying
new material will bomb.

00:05:39.868 --> 00:05:41.167
-Yes.
-That is an important part

00:05:41.167 --> 00:05:43.133
of the process.
-Yes.

00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:46.267
-But you would
sort of preplan for bombing.

00:05:46.267 --> 00:05:47.901
-[ Laughs ] Yes. Yes.

00:05:47.901 --> 00:05:50.367
-You would sort of just
have things ready to go in case.

00:05:50.367 --> 00:05:52.000
-Yeah, I was like,
I got to have lines

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:53.434
for when it doesn't go well

00:05:53.434 --> 00:05:56.334
because that is going to be
part of my journey, you know?

00:05:56.334 --> 00:05:59.000
So, like sometimes
if no one's saying anything,

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:01.767
I'd be like,
"Oh, you're laughing so hard,

00:06:01.767 --> 00:06:03.267
no sound is coming out.

00:06:03.267 --> 00:06:05.400
It's one of those laughs,
you know?"

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:08.601
Or "Oh, this is something we can
both journal about later,"

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:12.834
you know, like, just because,
you know, when you're bombing,

00:06:12.834 --> 00:06:14.167
it's like you're uncomfortable.

00:06:14.167 --> 00:06:17.133
But the audience is also
uncomfortable watching you fail.

00:06:17.133 --> 00:06:18.567
Like, it's not fun
for them either.

00:06:18.567 --> 00:06:19.534
-No.
-Yeah.

00:06:19.534 --> 00:06:21.000
-That is the thing.
-Yeah.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:22.601
-When you're like,
when people are like,

00:06:22.601 --> 00:06:23.767
"That was a bad audience,"
I'm like,

00:06:23.767 --> 00:06:27.100
"They were also having
a terrible time."

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.801
-And they paid to have
a terrible time,

00:06:29.801 --> 00:06:33.601
so they lost money
and had a bad time.

00:06:33.601 --> 00:06:35.033
-I was a guest.

00:06:35.033 --> 00:06:37.400
You did a podcast for Audible

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:39.133
called "Introverts:
A Survival Guide."

00:06:39.133 --> 00:06:40.934
-Yes.
-And I really enjoyed

00:06:40.934 --> 00:06:42.400
talking to you.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.200
Did you enjoy
doing that podcast?

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:46.367
Do you feel like --
Were you setting out

00:06:46.367 --> 00:06:48.067
to make a podcast
for people

00:06:48.067 --> 00:06:51.834
that were facing the same
difficulties you were having?

00:06:51.834 --> 00:06:53.300
-Yeah, I mean,
I'm an introvert

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:56.133
and I feel like
I've always lived very much

00:06:56.133 --> 00:06:58.534
from the inside out,
and I will say

00:06:58.534 --> 00:07:01.501
I mostly interviewed
introverts for the podcast.

00:07:01.501 --> 00:07:04.567
And then you were kind of
the surprise guest at the end

00:07:04.567 --> 00:07:06.000
where it was like,
surprise,

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.300
you're going to interview
an extrovert to learn some tips.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.467
And guess what.
It's a professional interviewer.

00:07:12.467 --> 00:07:13.734
Good luck!

00:07:13.734 --> 00:07:15.567
[ Laughter ]

00:07:15.567 --> 00:07:17.501
So yeah, it was
pretty much my nightmare.

00:07:17.501 --> 00:07:19.300
-Yeah, well, I had a great time.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:22.601
I want you to know it was not
my nightmare.

00:07:22.601 --> 00:07:25.267
The book is wonderful.
The podcast is also wonderful.

00:07:25.267 --> 00:07:26.400
And thank you so much
for being back.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:27.701
It is a joy to have you.

00:07:27.701 --> 00:07:29.667
-Thank you for having me.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:29.667 --> 00:07:31.534
-Aparna Nancherla, everybody.

00:07:31.534 --> 00:07:34.434
"Unreliable Narrator"
is on sale now.

00:07:34.434 --> 00:07:36.300
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

